120363203127855640@g.us	3B01F873525D222A80B0	ad7dd3fbf40ba873077617bd97559f5ab310069ac0d880ed494057d74dc3eeaf	Jami	2026-10-22 15:56:47+00	⚠️ Heads up: a serious website security flaw (for the next week or so)\n\nA major flaw was found in PHP, the software that runs a huge share of websites (most WordPress sites, small shops, forums, booking sites). The fix came out on 21 Oct, but every website has to install it, and many won't for days or weeks. Until then, hackers can break into unpatched sites and sometimes read the passwords people type in.\n\nYour phone and computer are fine. The risk is on websites. For the next week or two:\n\n1.⁠ ⁠Log in and pay only on big sites where you can. Amazon, your bank, and Google are fine. Be more careful with small shops, local business sites, forums and old accounts. If it can wait a week, wait.\n\n2.⁠ ⁠Pay at checkout with PayPal, Apple Pay or Google Pay instead of typing your card details into a small site.\n\n3.⁠ ⁠Don't reuse passwords. If you used the same password on a small site as on your email or bank, change the important ones now. A password manager makes this easy.\n\n4.⁠ ⁠Turn on two-step login (a text code or an authenticator app) for email, banking and social media. A stolen password alone then isn't enough to get in.\n\n5.⁠ ⁠Expect scam emails about this. Scammers will send fake "your account was hacked, reset your password here" messages. Don't click the links. Go to the site yourself or use its app.\n\n6.⁠ ⁠Watch your bank statements for a few weeks.\n\n7.⁠ ⁠If you run a website (WordPress, a blog, a small shop): updating WordPress or plugins is NOT enough. Ask your host whether PHP has been updated to a fixed version. Most big hosts are doing it automaticall	[]	\N	\N	2026-10-22 15:56:48.654608+00	live	+14162012407
